Pi-DOS CPU Setup
===============

Run PIDOSCFG at the DOS prompt to open the CPU profile selector.

Use Up/Down or Home/End to choose a profile. Tab and Shift+Tab move focus.
F2 or Alt+S saves. Escape, Alt+C or Alt+X cancels without writing anything.
Restore defaults (F3 or Alt+R) selects Pentium 133 MHz. Save to write the defaults;
Cancel still leaves the configuration unchanged.
You can also click a profile and click Save or Cancel. A mouse is optional.
Enter activates the focused control (and saves when a profile is selected).

Profiles:
  Automatic             Automatically adjust CPU execution speed
  286 12 MHz
  386DX 33 MHz
  486DX 33 MHz
  486DX2 66 MHz
  486DX4 100 MHz
  Pentium 100 MHz
  Pentium 133 MHz        Default for fresh images and Restore defaults
  Pentium MMX 200 MHz

MHz labels describe approximate performance equivalents, not exact clocks.
The Raspberry Pi must be fast enough to sustain the requested speed.
At boot, Date and Time appear on the left of the system table; the CPU
model appears on the right, directly above DOS video.

Saving updates only cputype, core and cycles in C:\SYSTEM\PIDOS.CFG.
Other settings and comments on separate lines are preserved. Existing CPU
values are replaced; missing CPU keys are added. Custom settings are shown
without changing them until you choose a profile and save.

Run REBOOT after saving to apply your changes. RAM and other settings are
not part of a CPU profile. The original file is backed up as PIDOS.BAK.
Saving again replaces that backup with the immediately previous version.
Read-only files are left unchanged. Files over 24 KiB cannot be edited.

If PIDOS.TMP exists after an interrupted save, check it and PIDOS.BAK before
deleting or renaming recovery files. Setup will not overwrite an existing
PIDOS.TMP. You can restore PIDOS.BAK to PIDOS.CFG from another computer.

Command-line examples:
  PIDOSCFG /LIST
  PIDOSCFG /PROFILE 486-66
  PIDOSCFG /CONFIG C:\SYSTEM\TEST.CFG
  PIDOSCFG /HELP

Program and source are original Pi-DOS work. Built with Open Watcom.
For the compiler runtime license and source information, see WATCOM.TXT
in this directory.
